WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.192 --> 00:00:01.807
(slow piano music)

00:00:01.807 --> 00:00:03.385
- [Narrator] This is Ruby,

00:00:03.385 --> 00:00:05.534
a burrowing owl.

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:07.229
These long-legged owls

00:00:07.229 --> 00:00:08.473
are found throughout

00:00:08.473 --> 00:00:10.092
North and South America.

00:00:10.092 --> 00:00:11.471
Due to habitat loss,

00:00:11.471 --> 00:00:13.971
their population is declining.

00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:20.503
Burrowing owls are medium-sized birds

00:00:20.503 --> 00:00:22.197
reaching a height of 10 inches

00:00:22.197 --> 00:00:24.226
and weighing up to half a pound.

00:00:24.226 --> 00:00:25.718
Brown-colored feathers

00:00:25.718 --> 00:00:28.348
with white spots compliment its long legs

00:00:28.348 --> 00:00:29.474
and short tail.

00:00:29.474 --> 00:00:30.981
They nest underground

00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:33.287
in abandoned burrows of prairie dogs

00:00:33.287 --> 00:00:34.737
or ground squirrels.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:36.791
Because of their ground level nesting,

00:00:36.791 --> 00:00:38.606
they are more subject to predators

00:00:38.606 --> 00:00:40.352
than other owl species.

00:00:40.352 --> 00:00:42.275
They prefer open grasslands,

00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:44.896
but have been know to nest in peculiar

00:00:44.896 --> 00:00:48.191
places like golf courses
or airport fields.

00:00:48.191 --> 00:00:51.354
But as their habitat continues to shrink,

00:00:51.354 --> 00:00:53.352
their population has declined

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:55.839
by a third in the last half century.

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:57.748
This is the burrowing owl.

00:00:57.748 --> 00:01:00.748
(slow upbeat music)

